text: "# LinkedIn Post Formats\n\nPick the structure that fits the idea, then write it in the brand voice at a LinkedIn register.\nEach delivers the post's payoff and sets up a real comment. Rotate them so a feed doesn't become\none-note.\n\nFor each: when to use, the shape, and the close.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Personal story → lesson\n**Use when:** you have a real experience with a professional takeaway. The highest-trust,\nhighest-dwell LinkedIn format — *when genuine*.\n**Shape:** drop into the moment → the tension/turn → the lesson, generalized for the reader.\n**Close:** \"Has this happened to you?\" / \"What would you have done?\"\n**Warning:** must be a real story. Manufactured emotional parables are the cringe everyone sees\nthrough (see `avoiding-cringe.md`).\n\n## 2. Contrarian / POV\n**Use when:** the brand has a defensible point of view (from brand-profile). Drives the best\ncomments.\n**Shape:** state the unpopular position plainly → back it with reasoning/experience → acknowledge\nthe nuance.\n**Close:** \"Where am I wrong?\" / \"Curious if anyone's seen the opposite.\"\n**Warning:** a *real* take, not manufactured controversy or ragebait.\n\n## 3. How-to / listicle\n**Use when:** you can teach something practical. High save value; great as a **document carousel**.\n**Shape:** promise the outcome → numbered, concrete steps/points → a takeaway.\n**Close:** \"What would you add?\" / \"Which of these do you skip?\"\n\n## 4. Case study / result\n**Use when:** you have a real result and can show the how. Authority + proof.\n**Shape:** the result up front (the number/outcome) → brief context → what you actually did → the\ntransferable principle.\n**Close:** \"Happy to go deeper on any step — what's most useful?\"\n**Compliance:** only verifiable results; respect client confidentiality and the brand's proof\nrules.\n\n## 5. Observation / insight\n**Use when:** you've noticed something true about your industry or work. Pure thought leadership.\n**Shape:** the sharp observation → why it's happening / why it matters → the implication.\n**Close:** \"Are you seeing this too?\"\n\n## 6. \"I did X — here's what happened\"\n**Use when:** an experiment, a change, a journey with a result. Curiosity + relatability.\n**Shape:** the thing you tried → the honest outcome (including what didn't work) → the lesson.\n**Close:** \"Worth trying in your world?\"\n\n## 7. Document-carousel post\n**Use when:** a how-to or framework that benefits from swiping. Highest dwell/saves.\n**Shape:** a short, strong text post (hook + promise + one line of context) that pulls people into\nthe PDF; the slides carry the depth. Hand the slides to `carousel-writer`.\n**Close:** \"Save this for later — and tell me what's missing.\"\n\n---\n\n## The comment-driven close (every format)\n\nLinkedIn reach is gated on early comments, so the close matters as much as the hook. End with a\n**genuine question**:\n\n- Relevant to the reader's actual work.\n- Easy to answer but interesting enough to want to.\n- Open-ended, not yes/no, and **never** \"Agree? 👇\" or \"Comment YES.\"\n\nThe best questions invite people to share their own experience — that's what produces the\nreal, reach-expanding comments (and gives you something to reply to). Pair it with at most **one**\nsoft CTA.\n"
